/* world/west/lore.inc -- Lorebook entries: [LORE_X] = { LCH_..., LSRC_..., "TITLE", "page", "talk" },. Owner: W-WEST. */
    [LORE_SALTWIND] = { LCH_PLACES, LSRC_SALT_RAKER, "SALTWIND TRAIL",
        "West of Mirror Lake the land runs out of trees and into dunes. The trail to PORT BRINE follows "
        "the old salt road over them, past the pans where the Vale's salt has been raked for three "
        "hundred years.\n\n"
        "A pan is just a shallow square of clay. Sea water is let in at high tide, the gate is shut, "
        "and the sun and the wind do the rest. As the water evaporates the brine gets stronger, until "
        "salt crystals grow on the surface and sink. Rakers pull them into heaps to drain and dry.\n\n"
        "REAL SCIENCE: this is solar evaporation, and it is how much of the world's sea salt is still "
        "made. Sea water is about 3.5 percent salt; the pans concentrate it about ten times before "
        "crystals appear.\n\n"
        "VALE ONLY: PEBBOTTER love the warm brine, and CRANICRAB pick the pans clean of flies.",
        "Rake, pull, heap. Rake, pull, heap. Want to know why the pans are square?\f"
        "So the wind can reach every corner. Salt is just sea water that the sun and the wind "
        "have argued with long enough." },
    [LORE_SEABIRDS] = { LCH_PLACES, LSRC_BIRDER, "SEABIRDS AND KIN",
        "The birdwatcher of Saltwind keeps two lists: birds, and kin that are mistaken for birds.\n\n"
        "Gulls ride the air that rises where the wind hits the cliffs. PUFFOWL do the same by day. "
        "STORMBRELA hop along the tide line on windy days, opening and shutting like a lost umbrella, "
        "and nobody has ever seen one fly on a calm day.\n\n"
        "REAL SCIENCE: seabirds really do soar on cliff updrafts and on the wind over waves "
        "(dynamic soaring), which lets an albatross cross an ocean while hardly flapping.\n\n"
        "VALE ONLY: GALE kin pump from those same updrafts, so they crowd the cliffs on stormy days.",
        "Shh! A STORMBRELA, over there by the rocks. It only opens when the wind is up.\f"
        "Birds soar on the air that climbs the cliffs. The kin figured that out long before we did." },
    [LORE_PORT_BRINE] = { LCH_PLACES, LSRC_FISHMONGER, "PORT BRINE",
        "PORT BRINE is the Vale's only harbour: a hill of blue and red roofs above granite quays, "
        "boats bobbing below and the lighthouse on the headland. Salt, fish and news all come in "
        "through Brine. So does the weather.\n\n"
        "The town grew up around the CURRENT HALL, an old bathhouse where TIDE kin were rested in "
        "running water. Today it is a Hall, and its Master tests wardens in the channels.\n\n"
        "The ferry to GULL ISLE sails from the western pier. The HARBOR OFFICE sells the crossing, "
        "and trusted helpers get a FERRY PASS.",
        "Fresh from the boats! Mackerel, sprats... that crab's not for sale, he just likes it here.\f"
        "Everything in Brine comes by sea. Even the gossip. Especially the gossip." },
    [LORE_TIDES] = { LCH_WEATHER, LSRC_SKIPPER, "THE TIDES",
        "Twice a day the sea climbs the quays of Port Brine and twice a day it slides away again.\n\n"
        "The Moon pulls on the whole Earth, but it pulls a little harder on the side facing it and a "
        "little softer on the far side. The oceans bulge on both sides, and as the Earth turns, every "
        "harbour passes through two bulges a day. When Sun and Moon line up (full and new moon) the "
        "tides are biggest: spring tides. At half moon they are smallest: neap tides.\n\n"
        "REAL SCIENCE: all of the above, and tides are slowly stretching our day, by about two "
        "thousandths of a second each century.\n\n"
        "VALE ONLY: TIDE kin pump from moving water, so they are liveliest on the spring tides, and "
        "fishers say brimming kin wash ashore at the full moon.",
        "Forty years at sea. You want to know the one thing I learned? The Moon runs the harbor.\f"
        "Two tides a day, bigger at full moon. The kin know it too. They come in on the spring tides." },
    [LORE_LIGHTHOUSE] = { LCH_PLACES, LSRC_LIGHTKEEPER, "THE BRINE LIGHT",
        "The lighthouse on the headland has kept ships off the rocks for two centuries. Its lamp is "
        "small; its lens is not. Rings of heartglass prisms, each tilted a little more than the last, "
        "bend the lamp's light into a single flat beam that can be seen from Gull Isle.\n\n"
        "REAL SCIENCE: this is a Fresnel lens, invented in 1822. Cutting a thick lens into thin rings "
        "keeps its focusing power but loses most of its weight, so lighthouse beams could suddenly "
        "reach over thirty kilometres.\n\n"
        "VALE ONLY: the lens is heartglass, grown slowly like lantern mirrors. The keeper says a "
        "sleepy JELLUME lives in the lamp room and brightens it on foggy nights.",
        "The light? It's not the flame that matters, it's the glass. Rings of prisms, all bending one "
        "way.\fOne little lamp, and ships see it from Gull Isle. And here, take these. Rock pools are "
        "full of tired TIDE kin." },
    [LORE_CURRENT_HALL] = { LCH_HALLS, LSRC_HALL_GUIDE, "THE CURRENT HALL",
        "The CURRENT HALL was built as a bathhouse for TIDE kin. Sea water is pumped up from the "
        "harbour and let run back down through stone channels. A TIDE kernel pumps from moving water, "
        "so a tired kin set in a channel wakes up fast.\n\n"
        "Every channel runs one way only. Step into one and it carries you until it spills you onto "
        "a platform. The Hall's puzzle is to choose the right channel and to set the sluice gates: "
        "each floor switch raises one tide of gates and lowers another, and the bell in the east chute "
        "rings a third tide every time the water carries you over it.\n\n"
        "Beat HALL MASTER MAREN to earn the TIDE CREST. With it, a strong TIDE kin (level 20 or more) "
        "can carry you across open water: SURF.",
        "Welcome to the CURRENT HALL! Tip one: the water only flows one way.\f"
        "Tip two: look where a channel ENDS before you jump in. Some end at MAREN. Some end right back "
        "here with me." },
    [LORE_TIDE_CREST] = { LCH_HALLS, LSRC_STORY, "THE TIDE CREST",
        "You beat HALL MASTER MAREN in the CURRENT HALL of PORT BRINE and earned the TIDE CREST: a "
        "wave of blue glass set in a silver ring.\n\n"
        "With the crest, a TIDE kin that can SURF (level 20 or more, and big enough to carry you) may "
        "take you across open water. The SEA ROUTE to GULL ISLE is open, and so are the bays and "
        "lakes all over the Vale. Kin on the water are different from kin on land, so look around.\n\n"
        "Crests are part of the Wardens' Accord. Six of them open the way to the Ossuary.",
        0 },
    [LORE_GULL_ISLE] = { LCH_PLACES, LSRC_ISLE_ELDER, "GULL ISLE",
        "GULL ISLE is a hump of sand, grass and palms at the end of the Sea Route. Eleven people live "
        "there, and far more gulls. The islanders dry fish, rake salt, and keep an eye on the sea cave "
        "under the south cliff.\n\n"
        "The cave has a name: the DROWNED BELL. Long ago, before the Kinship, the sea rose and "
        "swallowed a chapel on the old shore. Its bell was never found, but on still nights the "
        "islanders hear it ring below the cliff.",
        "Visitor? Sit down, sit down. The ferry brings one a week, if that.\f"
        "You'll have heard the bell by now. No? Wait for a still night. Then you'll hear it." },
    [LORE_GLOWING_SEA] = { LCH_BIOLOGY, LSRC_ODA, "THE GLOWING SEA",
        "On summer nights the water around Gull Isle glows blue where oars dip and waves break. In the "
        "sea cave the rocks glow all the time.\n\n"
        "REAL SCIENCE: this is bioluminescence. Tiny plankton (dinoflagellates) make light with a "
        "molecule called luciferin and an enzyme called luciferase; a jolt of the water makes them "
        "flash. Deep-sea animals use light to hunt, to hide and to talk.\n\n"
        "VALE ONLY: kin light is different. A kernel leaks light because its condensate is made half "
        "of light. Near the cave, the plankton flash in time with something much bigger breathing "
        "below.",
        "The sea glows blue when you stir it. Little plankton, lighting up when they're startled.\f"
        "But in the cave it glows by itself. It pulses. Slow. Like something breathing." },
    [LORE_DROWNED_BELL] = { LCH_LEGENDS, LSRC_ODA, "THE DROWNED BELL",
        "Before the Kinship, when the Vale burned and flooded, the sea took the old shore chapel and "
        "its bronze bell. The islanders say the bell still rings in the cave under Gull Isle, though "
        "nothing moves the water there.\n\n"
        "The Almanac has another explanation. Something very large sings in the deep channel off the "
        "island, and a bell of the right size rings along with it, the way a wine glass hums when you "
        "sing its note.\n\n"
        "REAL SCIENCE: this is resonance. A bell has notes it likes to ring at; sound at exactly that "
        "pitch makes it ring by itself.",
        "The bell rings on still nights. Not the wind. Not the waves.\f"
        "My grandmother said the bell answers someone singing, down where the sea is dark. Go and "
        "listen, warden. Carefully." },
    [LORE_NOCTHALE] = { LCH_LEGENDS, LSRC_ODA, "NOCTHALE, THE STAR WHALE",
        "Sailors of the Vale tell of NOCTHALE, a whale with a hide like the night sky, which sings "
        "under the sea on moonless nights. Its song, they say, pulls the tides in.\n\n"
        "The Almanac thinks NOCTHALE pumps from the faint rain of particles that falls from the stars "
        "through water and rock alike, which is why it can live so deep, and why it is so slow and "
        "so old.\n\n"
        "REAL SCIENCE: whale song really does carry across whole oceans. Deep down there is a layer "
        "of water (the SOFAR channel) that traps sound, and a whale call can travel thousands of "
        "kilometres through it.\n\n"
        "VALE ONLY: NOCTHALE itself. It is said to answer only a warden who waits in the bell cave.",
        "The star whale? Oh, it's real. My husband saw its back once, all speckled like the sky.\f"
        "Its song goes all the way round the sea. If it's brimming, the tides will go wrong. Somebody "
        "should answer it." },
    [LORE_REEDWICK] = { LCH_PLACES, LSRC_REEDWICK, "REEDWICK",
        "Reedwick stands on stilts above the fen. Rope bridges keep the hearth and smokehouse above "
        "the high water. The boatbuilders know every hidden channel.\n\n"
        "REAL SCIENCE: wetlands filter sediment and shelter young fish among their roots.",
        "The roofs need reeds every season. Bring me TIDEBERRIES while you're here." },
    [LORE_FEN_WISPS] = { LCH_BIOLOGY, LSRC_FEN_WISPS, "FEN WISPS",
        "At night DUSK kin gather where the herons sleep. They pump from the dim light reflected "
        "off still water, and vanish when the lamps come on.\n\n"
        "REAL SCIENCE: marsh lights can come from gases released as plants decay.",
        "Those little lights aren't lamps. Look again when it gets dark." },
    [LORE_SALT_CACHE] = { LCH_PLACES, LSRC_SALT_CACHE, "THE CLIFF CACHE",
        "The old lighthouse stored its supplies above the salt road. A thunderclap loosened a "
        "boulder over the only path; STRENGTH can move it aside.",
        "The old cache is behind this rock. Mind the climb." },
